From 7c5d37f46285fb29127bdbda3afd42a259029079 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: logdog2325 <loganjayfisher@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 03:09:14 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] PBR: auto-patch clean discs for online play

Pokemon Battle Revolution's online mode still works through Wiimmfi, but
only if the disc's hardcoded GameSpy hostnames are redirected. Until now
the PBR launcher required users to source an already-patched disc. Carry
the redirect ourselves instead, so a clean dump in any container format
(iso/wbfs/rvz/ciso/nfs) just works.

What the payload is
-------------------
A "patched" PBR disc is the stock disc with 30 strings rewritten inside
main.dol (section data5) and nothing else changed -- 824 bytes per region,
no code patches:

   25x  *.gs.nintendowifi.net        -> *.gs.wiimmfi.de
    4x  "https://"                   -> "http://"
    1x  NAS User-Agent "RVL SDK/1.0" -> "D-3-0"

XDNetplay/PbrWiimmfiData.h carries that table for RPBE01 and RPBP01. It is
machine-generated, and was checked byte-for-byte against the output of the
official Wiimmfi patcher (wit --wiimmfi) for RPBE01 and against a shipped
Wiimmfi-patched disc for RPBP01. There is no RPBJ01 payload -- Wiimmfi does
not carry PBR-JP -- so that region is detected and reported rather than
silently half-supported.

How it is applied
-----------------
As Riivolution <memory> patches. Those are written into RAM by
CBoot::BootUp() once the DOL is loaded, so the disc image itself is never
read-modify-written and every container format works with no conversion.
The Riivolution "offset" attribute is a memory address -- ApplyMemoryPatch()
writes to (m_offset | 0x80000000) -- so the absolute virtual addresses in
the table are used as-is.

The Patch structs are built directly rather than by generating XML and
calling ParseString(). RiivolutionParser.cpp's <memory> handling is exactly
"m_offset = offset attribute, m_value = hex of the value attribute", so the
two are equivalent, and building them in code removes a hex round trip and a
runtime parse that could fail silently.

BootManager::BootCore() calls into the helper for every disc boot, which
covers the Android PBR launcher, the Android game grid and the desktop game
list alike. It is placed after the existing riivolution_patches check so
that adding these patches does not also force MAIN_FAST_DISC_SPEED -- the
disc image is untouched, so there are no Riivolution load times to skip.
The patches are appended to BootParameters::riivolution_patches rather than
handed to AddRiivolutionPatches(), because that helper additionally wraps
the volume in a DirectoryBlobReader so <file> patches can rewrite the FST.
There are no file patches here, so the wrapper would buy nothing and cost a
full FST rebuild plus on-the-fly Wii partition re-encryption every boot.

Not double-applying
-------------------
ExamineDisc() reads the disc's DOL header, maps each patched address back to
a file offset and compares the 30 fields, so a pre-patched disc is detected
and skipped. That read is about a kilobyte, not the whole 4.5 MB DOL.

Applying anyway would in fact be harmless, which is why the fallback for an
unreadable main.dol is to patch regardless: every replacement is the new
string NUL-padded out to the length of the string it replaces, so writing it
over an image that already contains it stores byte-identical data. Verified
by applying the embedded table to a copy of the official Wiimmfi-patched
RPBE01 main.dol and diffing -- zero bytes changed. The same harness confirms
stock main.dol + this table == official patched main.dol exactly.

Launcher UI
-----------
- The disc row accepts a clean dump and says the Wiimmfi patch is applied
  automatically at boot. It now reports which region was found and calls out
  the Japanese disc as unsupported instead of claiming it is ready to play.
- The NAND row is a warning, not a blocker: offline play works without one,
  and Play is no longer gated on it. The text is explicit that online play
  needs a NAND backup from the user's own Wii, because a shared or generated
  NAND gets rejected and can get the user banned.
- The disc picker stays a folder picker. Android's game list is built by
  UICommon::FindAllGamePaths() over directories (Common/FileSearch.cpp only
  walks directories, including for SAF content URIs), so a single-file
  picker would not survive a rescan. The label now says which folder to pick.
